item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Claims about exact subjects are relied on for promotion, closure or release.",
        "Finite evidence may observe a weaker or different subject or another basis.",
        "Constructors may be probabilistic or otherwise fallible.",
        "Every boundary relying on a claim has a decision owner."
      ],
      "definition": "F6 is the source's claims, evidence, assessment and acceptance family, covering EVALUATION §§1–5. Its instance premises are work conditions, not commitments or consequences of finite capacity alone. Its foundation table traces consequences and separately identifies added choices and their owners; it does not itself decide them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 75,
          "quote": "They are conditions of the work\nthis family governs, not commitments, and do not follow from finite\ncapacity alone.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line": 73
        }
      ],
      "key": "claims_evidence_assessment_acceptance",
      "label": "Claims, evidence, assessment and acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A load-bearing claim identifies its exact subject, claimed property, governing basis, witness-to-claim relation, nearest excluded weaker property and falsifier. Its evidence has a bounded subject, population, quantifier and scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 82,
          "quote": "a load-bearing claim names its exact subject, the property claimed, the governing basis, the relation between witness and claim, the nearest weaker property it excludes and its falsifier before a witness is selected",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line": 82
        }
      ],
      "key": "load_bearing_claim",
      "label": "Load-bearing claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The semantic basis comprises constitutional and accepted-design relations defining a claim's meaning. The evidence basis comprises the exact artifacts, events, logs, tests and observations used to evaluate it. The state projection is a current view derived from admitted semantic and evidence bases. Semantic law, evidence, evaluator application and decision disposition are four distinguishable roles.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 141,
          "quote": "Semantic law defines what an evaluation means;\nevidence supplies observations; the evaluator applies the declared\nrelation; decision authority disposes. One actor may lawfully perform\nseveral; the record keeps them distinguishable.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line": 138
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_bases_and_roles",
      "label": "Three bases and four roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Structural conformance concerns closed syntax, types, identities, references, bases and mechanically decidable predicates. Semantic fidelity concerns meaning and materiality. Structural success is evidence for semantic review, not semantic review itself.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 164,
          "quote": "Structural conformance decides closed syntax, types, identities, references,\nbases and mechanically decidable predicates. Semantic fidelity decides\nmeaning and materiality. Structural success is evidence for semantic\nreview, not a substitute for it.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line": 161
        }
      ],
      "key": "structural_conformance_and_semantic_fidelity",
      "label": "Structural conformance and semantic fidelity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Independent assessment is assessment distinct from construction, grounded in authorship and evidence conditions. The constructor's self-review remains construction output. EVALUATION §3 defines independence; TICKETS owns required trigger boundaries and a governing basis may add boundaries.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 190,
          "quote": "This section decides what counts as independent assessment. Which\nboundaries require it is a policy choice of the Spec Method, owned by\n[TICKETS §7 Execution and verification authority separation](TICKETS.md#execution-and-verification-authority-separation-up-007);\na governing basis may add boundaries.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F3.%20Independent%20assessment",
          "start_line": 187
        }
      ],
      "key": "independent_assessment",
      "label": "Independent assessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Author identity identifies the party responsible for a realised subject's content. Actor identity identifies whoever performs an operation and is an identity, not an authority. Carrying another author's contribution does not change its authorship.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 220,
          "quote": "Author identity is the party responsible for the content of a realised\nsubject. It is distinct from actor identity, whoever performs one\noperation, which is an identity and not an authority",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F3.%20Independent%20assessment",
          "start_line": 217
        }
      ],
      "key": "author_and_actor_identity",
      "label": "Author identity and actor identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A review result carries one core result per declared predicate actually evaluated, together with exact subject and basis, consulted evidence, consequence-ranked findings, each finding's evidence and smallest bounded correction, residual uncertainty and invalidation conditions. A traceable finding differs from commentary.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 249,
          "quote": "A review returns one core result\n([FOUNDATIONS §4, Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results)) for each declared\npredicate it evaluated, with: the exact subject and basis, the evidence\nconsulted, the findings ranked by consequence, each finding's evidence and\nthe smallest bounded correction, the residual uncertainty, and the\ninvalidation conditions.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results",
          "start_line": 244
        }
      ],
      "key": "review_result",
      "label": "Review result"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A review returns findings with technical triage."
      ],
      "definition": "Technical triage is the assessor's evidence-bound assessment of each finding against the exact evaluated claim and basis. Severity grades consequence as evaluation evidence. Priority, current promotion-boundary effect and disposition belong to the decision owner and remain distinct. Triage payload classes complete, indeterminate and not_applicable classify triage only. The core review-result domain is satisfied, falsified, indeterminate, out_of_frame and invalid_basis.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 267,
          "quote": "Severity, the assessor's grade of consequence,\nis evaluation evidence. Priority, the effect on the current promotion\nboundary and the disposition are the decision owner's",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results%2FResult%20and%20triage%20projection",
          "start_line": 265
        },
        {
          "end_line": 283,
          "quote": "The payload classes `complete`,\n`indeterminate` and `not_applicable` classify the triage only; they are\nnot new results, dispositions, priorities or lifecycle states",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results%2FResult%20and%20triage%20projection",
          "start_line": 281
        }
      ],
      "key": "technical_triage",
      "label": "Technical triage and core results"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The decision owner disposes evaluation evidence at its owned boundary. Acceptance is that owner's disposition over the unchanged evaluated subject, limited to the named instance and interval. Human authority and an admitted bounded proxy remain distinct.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 314,
          "quote": "Acceptance is the decision owner's disposition over an unchanged subject;\nrecording it cannot widen it, and it exhausts only the instance and interval\nit names",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance",
          "start_line": 312
        }
      ],
      "key": "decision_owner_acceptance",
      "label": "Decision owner and acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The accepted subject is a successor."
      ],
      "definition": "The successor disposition record identifies the exact predecessor immutable cut and one semantic disposition for every predecessor claim capable of affecting the successor. An intentionally removed disposition records a decision by current Product authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 335,
          "quote": "the exact predecessor immutable cut\nthe successor evolves, and one semantic disposition for every predecessor\nclaim capable of affecting the successor.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance",
          "start_line": 332
        }
      ],
      "key": "successor_disposition_record",
      "label": "Successor disposition record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A finding is a review-reported defect against the exact evaluated claim. Blocking current promotion is a decision-owner act over a finding. Applicable hard stops bound the verdict; profiles and work carriers add domain triggers to the single blocking rule.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 346,
          "quote": "A finding is a defect a review reports against the exact claim it\nevaluated (§4). Blocking current promotion is the decision owner's act\nover that finding (§4, Result and triage projection). This section is the\none statement of when a finding can block; profiles and work carriers\nrefer to it and add only their domain triggers.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance%2FWhen%20a%20finding%20blocks%20promotion",
          "start_line": 342
        }
      ],
      "key": "finding_and_promotion_block",
      "label": "Finding and promotion block"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A realisation conforms to specification Σ when its interpreted behaviour satisfies Σ under a declared interpretation contract and independent judgment. Semantic conformance uses the governing semantic basis as Σ.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 390,
          "quote": "A realisation aⱼ conforms to a specification Σ when its interpreted behaviour\nsatisfies Σ under a declared interpretation contract (P1) and an independent\njudgment (P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line": 388
        }
      ],
      "key": "realisation_conformance",
      "label": "Conformance of realisations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The equivalence ladder is an unordered list of four separate claims: conformance; semantic equivalence of realisations, meaning identical interpreted behaviour on a stated domain including behaviour outside Σ; computational equivalence, meaning the same function at equal cost for declared workloads on architecture H; and categorical equivalence concerning categories and maps. Preservation of identities and composition is a further distinct claim. Here semantic equivalence does not mean concept equivalence across bounded contexts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 399,
          "quote": "The ladder is the list of the four claims named next,\neach a rung; the rungs are listed, not ordered, and no rung implies\nanother. Conformance, semantic equivalence, computational equivalence and\ncategorical equivalence are separate claims with separate evidence",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line": 395
        }
      ],
      "key": "equivalence_ladder",
      "label": "Equivalence ladder"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Layered sufficiency means a competent actor can derive a conformant next layer from each layer. Reconstructability requires a correct reconstruction route for the claim's span, rather than unique derivation or execution of that route on every use.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 431,
          "quote": "A specification arranged in layers is sufficient when\na competent actor can derive a conformant next layer from each layer; the\nderivation need not be unique, since different realisations may satisfy the\nsame upper layer, and the test is sufficiency, not determinism.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line": 428
        }
      ],
      "key": "layered_sufficiency",
      "label": "Layered sufficiency and reconstructability"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A task builds or assesses one successor member against its predecessor."
      ],
      "definition": "A reconstruction return separates semantic conservation, demonstrated reduction and mere relocation. Conservation retains a disposed predecessor claim's meaning, assessed by reading both texts against its disposition. Reduction is a lower-cost deciding path on a declared measure, evidenced by both path traces and declared tradeoffs. Relocation moves text between owners or carriers without changed meaning or a traced deciding-path change.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 469,
          "quote": "A reconstruction return is the return of a task that builds or assesses\none member of a successor against its predecessor. It states under three\nseparate heads what it claims and the evidence for each, and claims none of\nthem by a byte or size measure.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations%2FReturn%20of%20a%20reconstruction",
          "start_line": 466
        }
      ],
      "key": "reconstruction_return",
      "label": "Reconstruction return"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "One specification may have several realisations.",
        "Fallible constructors produce realisations, frames and methods relied on by other actors.",
        "Qualification uses finite cases run within declared actor envelopes."
      ],
      "definition": "F7 is the source's conformance, equivalence and qualification family, covering EVALUATION §§6–7 and CONFORMANCE. Its premises are work conditions, not commitments or consequences of finite capacity alone. It explicitly cites P0's actor-change premise: a later actor may reacquire declared sources but not private memory.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 499,
          "quote": "They are conditions of\nthe work this family governs, not commitments, and do not follow from\nfinite capacity alone. P0's actor-change premise, that the actor of one\nstep need not be the actor of the next and may re-acquire declared sources\nbut not private memory, is cited by name.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20conformance%2C%20equivalence%20and%20qualification",
          "start_line": 495
        }
      ],
      "key": "conformance_equivalence_qualification",
      "label": "Conformance, equivalence and qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The document's qualification division groups qualification of candidate frames and of the method itself. This heading supplies organization, not an additional qualification test.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 545,
          "quote": "## 7. Qualification of frames and of the method",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method",
          "start_line": 545
        }
      ],
      "key": "qualification_division",
      "label": "Qualification of frames and of the method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Frame qualification evaluates a candidate frame in proportion to its claims and binds the exact revision, actor envelope, evaluation family, basis class and evaluated evidence conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 578,
          "quote": "Qualification binds the exact frame revision, actor envelope, evaluation\nfamily, basis class and evidence conditions evaluated. It does not\ngeneralise to an unevaluated population by naming the frame generically",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FFrame%20qualification",
          "start_line": 575
        }
      ],
      "key": "frame_qualification",
      "label": "Frame qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Fit concerns whether the actor class can hold the frame: in the context dimension, selected representation size against declared capacity. Adequate attention concerns whether the actor class actually operates it adequately on declared tasks under declared conditions. They are separately evaluated capability questions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 587,
          "quote": "Whether the actor class\noperates the frame adequately is adequate attention, and only actual-use\nevidence establishes it",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FFrame%20qualification",
          "start_line": 585
        }
      ],
      "key": "fit_and_adequate_attention",
      "label": "Fit and adequate attention"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Method self-qualification applies the ordinary qualification method, consuming the work boundary, construction and assessment relation, and layered sufficiency without creating a second process. Its population comprises every claimed method function and failure relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 608,
          "quote": "The method is qualified the way it qualifies anything else. Qualification\nof generic method sufficiency consumes the work boundary (FOUNDATIONS §2),\nthe construction and assessment relation of ACTIVATION §1 and the layered\nsufficiency of §6; it creates no second process.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line": 605
        }
      ],
      "key": "method_self_qualification",
      "label": "Method self-qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The method qualification table names declare, activate, evaluate, conjoin, translate, audit_coverage, revise, result consumption and material composition as distinct relations requiring representative observations. These are described operations, not operations performed by this interpretation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 632,
          "quote": "The qualification population is every function and failure relation the\nmethod claims (REFERENCE_FRAME §5), each with its required representative\nobservations (P6):",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line": 630
        }
      ],
      "key": "method_relation_population",
      "label": "Method qualification relation population"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Derived guidance includes compressions, frame packets, skills and other method projections. Native use is an ordinary actor applying the guidance through its declared entry and available context.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 663,
          "quote": "Derived guidance. Changed derived guidance, a compression, a frame packet,\na skill or any other projection of the method, is qualified against\nsource-derived expected behaviour, including the affected permissions,\nexclusions and invalidators. For a native use claim, an ordinary actor\napplies that guidance through its declared entry and available context.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line": 659
        }
      ],
      "key": "derived_guidance",
      "label": "Derived guidance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A particular activation is selected for measurement."
      ],
      "definition": "The recoverability benchmark measures whether a fresh actor using only published documents recovers governing relations with independently judged fidelity, and its reconstruction cost, against the rc.2 corpus. It supplies F27/F28 observations; F29/F30 report the same observation with completed task, material errors detected and cost.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 693,
          "quote": "For a selected activation, measure whether a fresh actor given\nonly the published documents recovers the governing relations with fidelity\njudged independently, and at what reconstruction cost, against the rc.2\ncorpus as baseline.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line": 690
        }
      ],
      "key": "recoverability_benchmark",
      "label": "Recoverability benchmark"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A conformance case identifies its subject, tested mechanism, independently specified expected distinction and status describing what exists. A bounded-construction trial additionally declares per-actor context cap and total-work budget before each run.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 515,
          "quote": "a conformance case names its subject, the mechanism under test, an independently specified expected distinction and its status, and a status names what exists",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20conformance%2C%20equivalence%20and%20qualification",
          "start_line": 515
        }
      ],
      "key": "conformance_case_and_trial",
      "label": "Conformance case and bounded trial"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "EVALUATION's source-disposition tables report conservation and relocation claims: UP-001→§1; UP-022→§§1,3,6,7; UP-008/RF-007→§2; AC-018/AC-019→generic §2 with model bindings elsewhere; UP-007→independence §3 and blocking §5 with trigger/traceability ownership in TICKETS; RF-013 and frame/method qualification→§7; UP-013/AC-014/UP-004/UP-020 acceptance and proxy aspects→§5; UP-015 acceptance evidence→§5 with full law in RELEASE §9; Reconstruction Litmus→§6 with chain application elsewhere; judgment continuity→TICKETS and ACTIVATION; reviewer triage→§4 with profile-specific fields and steps retained in EXECUTIVE_BASELINE. The text identifies the equivalence ladder, attention separation, recoverability benchmark, foundation tables and generalized triage/blocking projections as new material, and author identity as relocated ownership. These are reported source claims, not verified conservation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 714,
          "quote": "| RF frame qualification, method self-qualification | conserved; §7; rows in the table below; the adequate-attention paragraph is new |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F8.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 714
        },
        {
          "end_line": 727,
          "quote": "Also new: the tables of families F6 (§1) and F7 (§6), in\nthe format of FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law; each decides nothing and\nnames the rule that states each consequence.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F8.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 725
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_source_dispositions",
      "label": "EVALUATION source-disposition ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
